WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.920
Welcome to unintended consequences, Episode 28 for June 11th, 2026.

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:10.280
Today's story is one of those rare examples where the cure was almost worse than the disease,

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:11.920
at least at first.

00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:19.400
On the morning of March 13th, 2016, emergency rooms in Michigan recorded a 24% jump in

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:22.200
heart attack admissions compared with the previous Monday.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:27.820
That single day spike stood out because nothing else in the surrounding calendar had changed.

00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:34.000
No holiday, no weather event, no shift in medical staffing, the only alteration in the

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:38.700
preceding 24 hours had been the one hour advance of the clocks for daylight saving time.

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:43.220
Researchers later traced the same pattern in other years and other states,

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:46.240
confirming that the jump was not an isolated anomaly.

00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:51.980
The policy itself had been sold, first in 1916, and again in 1974,

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:57.220
as a way to stretch scarce fuel supplies by moving an hour of daylight into the evening.

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:00.960
The underlying arithmetic seemed straightforward at the time.

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:05.100
If households and factories needed less artificial light after sunset,

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:10.020
then the fuel that would have been burned for lamps or bulbs could be redirected elsewhere.

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:16.300
Instead, the same one hour shift now reliably produced measurable increases in myocardial

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:22.540
infarctions, fatal traffic accidents, and workplace injuries once the data were examined decades later.

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:29.620
The first nationwide adoption occurred in Germany and Austria-Hungary on 30 April, 1916.

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:36.380
The Imperial German government faced an allied blockade that had cut off overseas coal and food,

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:39.540
leaving domestic reserves under intense pressure.

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:45.700
Every ton of fuel burned for lighting was therefore a ton unavailable for the war economy.

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:49.100
Specifically for the blast furnaces that produced steel,

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:51.980
or the locomotives that moved troops and supplies.

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:58.380
Officials calculated that shifting clocks forward one hour would reduce evening demand for artificial

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:04.280
light, and thereby free coal for the steel mills and railroads, an assumption grounded in the

00:02:04.280 --> 00:02:08.040
simple observation that most lighting loads occurred after sunset.

00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:13.860
The same logic reappeared in the United States during the 1973 oil embargo.

00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:20.460
President Nixon asked Congress for emergency authority to extend daylight saving time year-round,

00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:26.380
arguing that an extra hour of evening daylight would cut national petroleum consumption by roughly

00:02:26.380 --> 00:02:33.500
1%. Lawmakers facing gas lines and rationing plans passed the emergency daylight saving time

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:40.500
Energy Conservation Act in December 1973, with broad bipartisan support because the projected

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:44.480
saving, though modest, looked achievable without new infrastructure.

00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:52.460
Germany's 1916 decree took effect in a single night, clocks advanced at 11 pm on 30 April,

00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:57.940
and the change was presented as a patriotic duty that every household could perform by moving

00:02:57.940 --> 00:03:05.120
the hands on its own timepieces. The United States followed in March 1918 under the Standard Time Act,

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:09.940
though the measure was repealed after the armistice, once the wartime pressure eased.

00:03:10.420 --> 00:03:18.140
During the 1973 crisis, the new federal rule began on 6 January 1974, and was scheduled to last

00:03:18.140 --> 00:03:23.360
until the end of the embargo, relying on the precedent that British gas lighting loads had fallen

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:29.840
measurably during both world wars when clocks were advanced. Skeptics in Congress, including

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:34.880
Senator Bob Dole, warned that children would be walking to school in darkness, and that the

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:39.360
energy savings might prove illusory once heating and cooling patterns were considered.

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:44.880
One might ask whether the extra evening light would simply offset any morning losses,

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:50.720
yet the objection centered on the fact that heating systems often run on different schedules than

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:55.740
lighting, and that air conditioning demand could rise in the newly lit evening hours.

00:03:56.620 --> 00:04:02.860
Large-scale studies later quantified the health effects, a 2008 Swedish registry study

00:04:02.860 --> 00:04:10.140
of more than 1.5 million heart attack cases found a 5% increase in incidents during the first week

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:15.880
after the spring transition. Similar spikes appeared in Michigan and Texas data sets,

00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:21.400
each showing the same directional rise even though the absolute numbers varied with local population

00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:28.640
size. The causal pathway runs through sleep deprivation. The body's circadian clock advances more

00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:35.020
slowly than the calendar, producing an average loss of 40-60 minutes of sleep on the night of the change.

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:42.260
That 40-60 minute deficit is not spread evenly. It concentrates among people whose internal rhythms

00:04:42.260 --> 00:04:48.140
are already near the threshold of vulnerability, raising blood pressure and inflammatory markers,

00:04:48.140 --> 00:04:52.140
and thereby elevating cardiac risk for those already susceptible.

00:04:53.300 --> 00:04:58.560
Traffic data show a parallel pattern. The National Highway Traffic Safety Administration

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:05.560
recorded a 6-7% rise in fatal crashes on the Monday after spring forward in states that observe

00:05:05.560 --> 00:05:11.860
the shift. One could object that drivers might adapt quickly, yet the data indicate the combined

00:05:11.860 --> 00:05:17.560
effect of darker morning commutes and reduced alertness persists long enough to register in the

00:05:17.560 --> 00:05:24.740
crash statistics for that single day. Energy savings proved negligible. A 2008 Department of Energy

00:05:24.740 --> 00:05:32.960
Report on the 2007 extension of daylight saving time found a 0.03% reduction in electricity use.

00:05:33.740 --> 00:05:39.400
That figure was statistically indistinguishable from zero once air conditioning loads in the evening

00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:44.400
were counted because the extra hour of daylight pushed cooling demand higher in regions where

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:50.440
temperatures remained elevated after sunset. In warm climates the extra hour of evening light

00:05:50.440 --> 00:05:56.140
increased cooling demand more than it reduced lighting demand, reversing the original wartime

00:05:56.140 --> 00:06:02.300
assumption that lighting reductions would dominate. By the 2010s, a growing number of jurisdictions

00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:09.960
began reversing course. Russia abandoned the practice in 2014 after three years of year-round daylight

00:06:09.960 --> 00:06:16.220
saving time produced widespread complaints of winter darkness in the morning. The European Parliament

00:06:16.220 --> 00:06:24.000
voted in 2019 to end seasonal clock changes by 2021, though implementation stalled, among member

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:29.880
states as each weighed its own latitude and commuting patterns. In the United States, Arizona

00:06:29.880 --> 00:06:35.880
except the Navajo Nation and Hawaii have never observed daylight saving time, while Florida and

00:06:35.880 --> 00:06:41.400
several other states have passed laws to remain on daylight time permanently if Congress permits.

00:06:42.280 --> 00:06:47.500
No large jurisdiction has reported new problems from staying on standard time year-round.

00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:54.360
Complex physiological and behavioral systems often absorb a simple schedule change by shifting risk

00:06:54.360 --> 00:07:00.800
rather than eliminating cost. When an intervention is justified solely by an assumed first order

00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:07.200
resource saving, any second order affects on sleep, commuting or temperature control can erase the

00:07:07.200 --> 00:07:14.400
intended benefit. Because the original wartime justification no longer applies, the policy now persists

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:20.960
largely through institutional inertia. The practical question for any proposed clock change is therefore

00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:26.840
whether the measurable health and safety externalities have been priced into the decision at all.

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:33.380
Consider a modern proposal to adjust school start times for energy savings. What second order affects

00:07:33.380 --> 00:07:39.280
on sleep and safety would you examine before supporting the change? That's unintended consequences

00:07:39.280 --> 00:07:44.580
for today. If this episode gave you something to think about, share it with a friend who'd appreciate

00:07:44.580 --> 00:07:49.340
the story. Subscribe wherever you listen and we'll be back tomorrow with another case.

